Classify each fragment. This is exactly the shape of the exam question.

**(a)**
```c
void housekeeping(void) {
    open_log_file();
    reset_counters();
    set_system_clock();
    load_configuration();
}
```
Nothing here shares data and nothing feeds anything else — the four calls are grouped only because they all run at start-up. **Temporal cohesion.**

**(b)**
```c
double stats(double a[], int n, int which) {
    if (which == 1) return mean(a, n);
    if (which == 2) return variance(a, n);
    return maximum(a, n);
}
```
Two readings, and the exam expects you to choose by the *dominant* feature. Because a flag selects among functions of the same general kind, this is **logical cohesion** — the classic signature is a parameter that switches between alternatives. (Had the module computed mean, variance and maximum of `a` and returned all three, it would be **communicational**, since every element works on the same data.)

**(c)**
```c
char *report(struct Employee *e) {
    return format_name(e->first, e->last);
}
```
The caller passes a whole `Employee` structure but `report` reads only two of its fields. Passing a composite structure of which only part is used is **stamp coupling**. Passing `e->first` and `e->last` directly would reduce it to **data coupling**.

**(d)**
```c
int total_pay;              /* global */
void add_bonus(void)  { total_pay += bonus(); }
void add_salary(void) { total_pay += base();  }
```
Two modules read and write the same global. **Common coupling.**

**(e)** Module `A` calls `B(data, VERBOSE)` and inside `B` the constant `VERBOSE` chooses between two processing branches.
The second parameter is not data, it is an instruction about how `B` should behave. **Control coupling.**

**(f)** `lexer()` produces a token stream which `parser()` consumes and turns into a tree which `codegen()` walks.
Each stage's output is the next stage's input, so as a single module this is **sequential cohesion**; viewed as separate modules connected only by the stream they pass, the coupling between them is **data coupling** — and that combination (sequential-to-functional cohesion with data coupling) is what a pipe-and-filter architecture buys you.

**Ranking check.** Order (a), (b), (f) by increasing cohesion: logical (2) < temporal (3) < sequential (6), so **(b) < (a) < (f)**. Order (c), (d), (e) by increasing coupling: data-structured/stamp (5) is looser than control (4), which is looser than common (2), so increasing coupling gives **(c) < (e) < (d)**.
